the parents of a teenage girl who died  from an allergic reaction have set up a  groundbreaking clinical trial to  investigate treatments for those with  allergies natasha eden namparooz died  after a severe reaction to a baguette  bought from a pret emoji store back in  2016. she was allergic to sesame it  wasn't listed on the ingredients at the  time as was the law we'll get into the  details of that in a moment and  natasha's parents nadim and tanya edmond  laperus are here and to speak about this  promising clinical trial good to see you  both um i know how hard you've been  working behind the scenes for so long to  get to this point and nadine perhaps you  can tell us about this trial at its  starting point  yeah so the natasha clinical trial is is  a real game-changing moment  in the uk for people with food allergies  it is basically a huge trial across five  university hospitals from the north of  england down to the south of england  where we're going to show  that actually you can use normal shop  bought foods  to be desensitized to the very foods  that you're allergic to  and therefore  onwards from the trial under clinical  supervision you can be you'll be able to  eat in fact the very foods that you were  allergic to and that were actually  inadvertently poison for you when now  the food will become in fact the remedy  for your for your food allergy it's an  amazing moment it's a traditional  science that dates back about 100 years  and what we're doing is we're just  bringing this science essentially into  the modern moment  and doing it at scale to show that it  can be done as a very cost effective way  for the nhs to treat millions of people  in the uk  yeah tanya i'm no scientist i'm no  expert on clinical trials but just  reading the background to this it does  sound like such a simple idea and as  adam says this has been tried and tested  before it's basically introducing  things that people are allergic to like  milk and peanuts which are you know some  of the more common allergies  introducing them slowly into someone's  diet to build up a tolerance is that  correct  that's right and over about a year you  take incremental tiny amounts and it  goes up a little bit and your immune  gets used to it and you actually get to  a point where you can tolerate  quite a lot of that allergen without  reacting  and  it's like like nad said it's something  that's been around for a long time um  but it could make such a big difference  because if we think of people with food  allergies and the smallest amount of  food can actually hospitalize them or  even kill them we don't with this um  treatment we don't have to worry about  may contain labels we don't have to  worry about  cross-contamination and and they can  actually even eat that food every single  day to stay out of allergy yeah um nadia  obviously the goal here is to save lives  but it's also to change lives as well  because obviously you lived with this  because of natasha and her allergy to  sesame  and  she had to be very careful when she ate  out and she was literally making life or  death decisions so just talk to me about  if this was to be successful how  life-changing it would be for hundreds  of thousands of people because food  allergies are much more common than many  people realize  that's right it's just really important  for your listeners and the viewers to to  know that food allergy is a disease of  the immune system  and it not to confuse us with  vegetarianism or veganism which are  lifestyle choices  and we're talking about food allergy  which can kill you or seriously  hospitalize you and the numbers of  people who are food allergic in the uk  and indeed in most developed countries  of the world are shooting up to  and also not in terms of  the numbers alone but what they're  allergic to so they're now people are  allergic to so many different foods when  i think when we were all children  growing up maybe we knew somebody who  was allergic to peanuts but now it's  moved on a lot and what we're really  going to show here is just how dramatic  and positive effect it can be on on  families  on their day-to-day lives that revolve  around food because after all we all  have to eat in order to live by actually  being able to eat the food that you're  allergic to  you are brought back into the normal  food economy  and you're not reliant on any form of  drugs or some kind of pharmaceutical if  you like it because food is the remedy  so food becomes the medicine and you can  participate in all the happy things the  families do like eating out or enjoying  family meals together rather than  avoiding them entirely  um  taney can i just say what a legacy you  are creating for your daughter natasha  our viewers will be well familiar uh  with natasha's law that came into effect  in october it was brought in making  allergy information a requirement for  food made on site because obviously  uh the food product that natasha ate was  made in-house on a premises and at the  time the law uh was such that you didn't  have to list all the ingredients and  you've changed that  and now you are doing more you're not  stopping  tanya this is enormous what you're doing  and all in natasha's name  yes it is in her name and um i think  that the turning point for us was after  natasha's inquest um we had no idea that  the numbers the biologies people with  allergies being diagnosed with them  had gone up in the last two decades as  they had done and we received so many  letters from families saying what's  happened to you is what we fear the most  and we live in fear every day  and so we know that natasha's law closed  a loophole in the law and that was  really important to give people clarity  and information about the food they're  eating  but really you know there's so much more  that needs to be done and this will  directly impact a lot of people and just  take that um  the terrible stress that people with  food allergies live with and we hope  that will take it away so they can just  have more hope of leading more normal  life  um  i think  we want to say that that's so important  so important that the idea of normal  lives um i think for people who don't  have food allergies they have no idea  just how stressful life is for families  who have children with allergies because  they live in fear that we  are the family you know that have the  worst tragedy story that that could  happen to them and and we you know tanya  and i have said along with our son alex  that we're going to dedicate  really the rest of our lives to try and  helping people with food allergies and  natasha's trial is a big big sort of  move towards just doing that  well it is incredible work you're doing  as you've said in natasha's name  and so many families around the world  will be looking at you and thanking you  for going to such efforts because as you  said it is  terrifying for families and isolating as  well for so many children growing up and  nadine and tania eden lamparooz thank  you so much for talking to us here on  sky news and best of luck with the  clinical trial we hope to catch up with  you soon about it thank you  thank you as well much appreciated thank  you
 

